    "title": "Six Years Later, the Highest Chart Peak of the Band's Career",
    "content": "Released on March 8, 2024 through Epic Records (Columbia in Japan two days earlier), *Invincible Shield* is Judas Priest's nineteenth studio album and Scott Travis's first Priest studio record since 2018's *Firepower*. It debuted at #2 on the UK Albums Chart — the highest chart position Judas Priest has ever reached in their home country — and opened at #18 on the Billboard 200 while topping Billboard's Top Hard Rock Albums chart. The album's centerpiece single, \"Crown of Horns,\" earned Judas Priest a nomination for the Grammy Award for Best Metal Performance in 2025, extending a run of Grammy recognition that now spans *Redeemer of Souls* (2014), *Firepower* (2018), and *Invincible Shield* (2024).\n\nFor a band fifty-plus years into its existence, and for a drummer thirty-five years into his tenure, *Invincible Shield* is a remarkable outlier: not a victory lap, but the highest-charting album of the band's career. Scott Travis — who joined Priest in 1989 and redefined the band's drumming vocabulary on *Painkiller* the following year — remains the connective tissue across every era this arc represents, from the speed-metal explosion of 1990 through the reunion record *Angel of Retribution* (2005) to this 2024 high-water mark.\n\n*Invincible Shield* was produced primarily by Andy Sneap, continuing the partnership established on *Firepower*, with Tom Allom returning to co-produce the album's closing two tracks, \"Sons of Thunder\" and \"Giants in the Sky.\" The record's production story is unusual for a modern metal album: rather than tracking together in a single studio, the band worked in near-total geographic isolation. Travis recorded his drum parts alongside guitarist Richie Faulkner in Nashville, Tennessee. Rob Halford tracked vocals separately in Phoenix, Arizona. Bassist Ian Hill contributed his parts while on tour in Europe. Sneap then assembled the pieces and re-amped guitar parts at his own Backstage Studios in England. The album that resulted sounds cohesive and heavy despite never having all four core members in the same room during tracking — a testament to Sneap's production and to how tightly Travis's foundational drum tracks locked the arrangements together for everyone recording around them.\n\nGear-wise, *Invincible Shield* documents Travis on the same long-standing ddrum Dominion Series kit and Paiste RUDE/2002 cymbals he's used since *Painkiller*, the same rig he used on *Firepower* six years earlier. This article covers that setup in full and what made his drumming on this era-defining record land as hard as it did.",
