    "title": "A Band Recorded Apart, Locked Together by the Drums",
    "content": "*Invincible Shield*'s production story is one of the more unusual in modern metal. Rather than assembling in one studio, Judas Priest tracked the album in near-total geographic separation. Scott Travis recorded his drum parts alongside guitarist Richie Faulkner in Nashville, Tennessee. Rob Halford tracked his vocals independently in Phoenix, Arizona. Bassist Ian Hill contributed his parts while on tour in various European cities. Producer Andy Sneap then assembled every element and re-amped the guitar parts at his own Backstage Studios in England.\n\n**Why the Drums Mattered More Than Usual:**\nIn a distributed production like this, the drum tracks function as the skeleton every other performance is built around. Travis's parts were recorded first and became the timing and arrangement reference for Faulkner's guitars in the same Nashville sessions, then for Halford's vocals in Phoenix and Hill's bass parts recorded on the road. A looser or less consistent drum performance would have made the eventual assembly audibly disjointed. Travis's decades of session discipline — the same reliability that let producers from Chris Tsangarides through Roy Z track him in complete takes — made the distributed approach work.\n\n**Sneap's Assembly Role:**\nAndy Sneap's job on *Invincible Shield* extended beyond traditional production into something closer to remote-session engineering: matching feel and tone across performances captured in three different cities and on tour, then re-amping guitars at Backstage Studios to unify the guitar tone regardless of where a given part was originally tracked. Travis's long-standing ddrum Dominion Series kit and Paiste RUDE/2002 cymbals gave Sneap a bright, well-defined drum signal that translated cleanly regardless of what was layered around it later.\n\n**The Result:**\nDespite the fractured recording process, *Invincible Shield* sounds like a band playing together in a room — a credit to both Sneap's assembly work and to Travis's drum tracks holding every other part in place.",
