    "title": "The Highest Peak, Thirty-Five Years In",
    "content": "*Invincible Shield* arrived in March 2024 as proof that Judas Priest's creative and commercial ceiling hadn't been reached decades ago. A #2 UK chart debut — the band's best ever — a Grammy nomination for \"Crown of Horns,\" and the third consecutive Priest album to earn major awards recognition: these are not the metrics of a legacy act coasting on nostalgia.\n\nFor Scott Travis, *Invincible Shield* closes a remarkable arc. From the ddrum Dominion Series shell pack that powered *Painkiller*'s speed-metal revolution in 1990, through *Angel of Retribution*, *Nostradamus*, *Redeemer of Souls*, and *Firepower*, to the same setup documented here on *Invincible Shield* — the same long-standing ddrum and Paiste rig anchors every chapter of Priest's history Travis has played on.\n\nWhat's remarkable about *Invincible Shield* is that it required Travis to do something he'd never done before: hold an entire album together while most of his bandmates recorded their parts in different cities and countries. The drums became the skeleton the rest of the record was built around, and the album's cohesion — despite that fractured process — is a direct credit to his reliability behind the kit.\n\nThirty-five years after replacing Dave Holland, Scott Travis is still the reason Judas Priest's rhythm section sounds inevitable. *Invincible Shield* didn't just extend his tenure — it gave him the highest chart peak of his career on the same long-standing ddrum and Paiste rig he's trusted from the start."
